{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nCloud and Jira DC currently nest retries across subprocess, SDK, wrapper, and reconciler layers. A non-idempotent ticket write can therefore exceed a predictable request budget or be replayed after an ambiguous commit. This task defines the provider-neutral logical-operation contract before either adapter changes.\n\n## Approach\nCreate immutable operation/outcome values and one fake-clock retry-budget owner in the reconciler domain. Adapter layers will report one physical invocation at a time; they will not sleep or replay. This keeps the approved retry envelope in one place while preserving the additive-jitter behavior already accepted in ADR 0084.\n\nThe contract is exact:\n- `logical_id` is one canonical UUID string generated once per logical operation and retained across every invocation and observation.\n- `disposition`, `failure_scope`, `replay_safety`, and `delay_source` serialize as fixed lowercase enum strings. Invocation and request totals are non-negative integers. Durations serialize as integer milliseconds. `retry_not_before` is UTC RFC 3339 with `Z`.\n- Canonical bytes are UTF-8 JSON with sorted keys, compact separators, and no ASCII escaping. This makes equivalent outcomes byte-identical.\n- `invocation_count` is the number already executed and ranges from 0 through 3. `retry_index` is zero-based: index 0 selects `[2s,3s)` and index 1 selects `[4s,5s)` using `2**(retry_index+1)+U[0,1)`.\n- Diagnostics are chronological immutable entries with only `stage`, `category`, `status_code`, `provider_code`, `retry_after_ms`, and `message`. Raw headers, request/response bodies, credentials, and ticket content are never admitted. Strings use the allowlist-first redaction classes documented by ADR 0041. Each message is capped after redaction at 512 Unicode code points, with an overlong value replaced by its first 511 code points plus `…`. The outcome holds at most eight entries; on overflow it retains the first seven and uses entry eight as `(stage=diagnostic, category=truncated, dropped=N)`. Thus diagnostics remain bounded and deterministic without depending on the currently unbounded LLM sanitizer implementation.\n- The budget permits at most three physical invocations and at most 15,000 ms cumulative sleep. An authoritative provider delay is a lower bound, never shortened. If the next required delay cannot fit, return `retry_not_before` without sleeping and without incrementing either invocation or request totals.\n- After an ambiguous write, observe the desired primary-store postcondition before replay. Observed desired state is recovered success; conclusive old state may replay if budget remains; failed or inconclusive observation is non-replaying `commit_unknown`.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/operation_outcome.py` — immutable provider-neutral values, bounded diagnostics, and canonical serialization.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/retry_budget.py` — sole three-invocation, 15-second fake-clock budget.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/test_operation_retry_budget.py` — deterministic contract, bounds, schedule, deadline, and ambiguity tables.\n- `docs/adr/0100-reconciler-operation-coordination.md` — provisional ownership and recovery decision.\n- `docs/adr/README.md` — ADR index entry.\n\nGrounding: `docs/adr/0084-acli-429-rate-limit-backoff.md` and `acli_subprocess.py::_rate_limit_backoff` establish the existing additive-jitter formula. `docs/adr/0041-llm-diagnostic-sanitization.md` and `rebar.llm.failure.sanitize_diagnostic` establish allowlist-first redaction, but inspection shows that implementation does not enforce the ADR size promise, so this operation contract states its own bound.\n\n## Testing\nUse injected UUID, jitter, and fake clock values. Table tests cover canonical byte serialization, Unicode truncation, diagnostic overflow, all budget boundaries, provider delays that fit or exceed the remaining sleep budget, and every ambiguity branch. Poison sleepers and invocation counters prove that rejected delays and `commit_unknown` perform no hidden work.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Operation/outcome objects expose the named provider-neutral fields and produce byte-identical sorted-key compact UTF-8 JSON for equivalent values; verified by contract serialization tests.\n- [ ] Diagnostic allowlisting, redaction, 512-code-point message truncation, and the eight-entry overflow sentinel match the exact contract above; verified by table tests containing credentials, Unicode, raw bodies, and nine entries.\n- [ ] One canonical logical id remains stable while invocation and request totals report only work actually executed; verified by a three-invocation fake sequence.\n- [ ] No schedule permits more than three physical invocations or 15,000 ms cumulative sleep; verified at exact and one-millisecond-over boundaries.\n- [ ] Zero-based retry indexes produce `[2s,3s)` and `[4s,5s)` and no third sleep; verified with deterministic jitter endpoints.\n- [ ] An authoritative provider delay is honored as a lower bound when it fits, while an over-budget delay returns `retry_not_before` without sleep or count increments; verified with fake-clock tables.\n- [ ] Ambiguity tables distinguish recovered success, remaining-budget safe replay, and non-replaying `commit_unknown`; verified with desired, old, failed, and inconclusive observations.\n- [ ] All time-dependent tests complete without wall-clock sleep and poison callbacks prove forbidden work is absent.\n- [ ] ADR 0100 records retry ownership, Jira non-idempotency, observe-before-replay, lifecycle owners, selected-route-only deltas, the retained ADR 0084 additive-jitter schedule, and why full/decorrelated jitter was not selected for only two bounded sleeps; verified by ADR numbering/index tests and document inspection.\n\n## Out of scope\nCloud and DC adapter execution, legacy dispatch integration, fuse consumption, and production cutover are later tasks."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787029776753933001,"uuid":"2d4277b9-27b8-4695-8714-addb8b90b434"}